;Kii Prefecture / Nankai
Nankai reported a record 52,000 hikers in June on the trails of Mount Kitakamine, Yagikoshu's highest peak at 3,298 metres. The Kii Prefecture Parks Service approved new trailhead restroom facilities to handle the growing numbers.

;Mito Prefecture / Fukuchiyama
Fukuchiyama approved a ¥180 million irrigation reservoir project along the Mito-gawa, the nation's fourth-longest river at 88 kilometres. The reservoir will serve 600 hectares of rice paddies. The local agricultural cooperative said the river is reliable but that reliability still requires infrastructure.

;Nagayama Prefecture / Tomioka
Tomioka reported a 10% increase in manufacturing output, with the Nagayama prefectural government noting that the Akamatsu Group collapse had minimal impact on the local industrial base. The town sits near Mount Rantakagadake and along the Nagayama-gawa, the nation's third-longest river.

;Sakai Prefecture / Kaizuka
Kaizuka reported a 25% increase in rooftop solar adoption, with 400 households installing panels in 2026. The Sakai Prefecture green energy subsidy programme covers roughly 30% of installation costs.

;Tosa Prefecture / Fukumoto
Fukumoto reported a 30% increase in citrus exports, with the local Tosa variety gaining popularity in Briesland and Echia. The cooperative said demand has outpaced supply and is exploring expanded orchard leases.
